# Vulnerabilities govulncheck reports that this repo knowingly ships with.
#
# One ID per line, with the reason. Anything NOT listed here fails the
# Vulnerability scan workflow, so this file is the whole difference between a
# gate and a warning nobody reads. Adding an entry is a decision; removing one
# is what happens when a fix ships.
#
# Every entry must record: what it is, why it cannot be fixed, and what would
# let it be deleted.

# --- github.com/jackc/pgx/v4 and pgproto3/v2 -------------------------------
#
# Both are denial-of-service issues with NO FIX in the v4 line (govulncheck
# reports "Fixed in: N/A"); pgx v4 is in maintenance and v5 is the current
# major. Reachable only through pkg/io/postgres_input.go — the PostgreSQL CDC
# consumer, which is configured entirely from POSTGRES_INPUT_* environment
# variables, is not a node type in the management API, and cannot be reached
# from any pipeline a tenant can build (see #239). It runs in docker-compose as
# a load-harness fixture and is not deployed to production at all:
# deploy-connectors-azure.sh does not mention it.
#
# Removed by either migrating pgx v4 -> v5, or resolving what the postgres CDC
# worker is for. The second is the open question; the first is a day's work
# that only matters if the answer is "keep it".
GO-2026-5004
